IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `eia-core`

-- Employment Insurance Regulations (SOR/96-332), regulations 11.1 to 13: Adjustment of Amount of Self-employed Earnings, and the
-- Average Number of Weeks for the purposes of paragraphs 7.1(6)(b) and 152.07(3)(b) of the Act. Piece er1, layer 1.
-- Text: the unofficial plain-text rendering of the Justice Laws consolidation current to 2026-09-30
-- (../../registers/source-bundle/SOR-96-332.txt). Nothing here is verified against the official text. `src:N` is a line of that file.
-- Instrument: the Regulations. The Act's subparagraph 152.07(1)(d)(i) (the amount of self-employed earnings, piece ei5),
-- paragraph 4(2)(b) (the ratio; the core holds the Act's s 4 but exposes no rule for the ratio, see NOTES.md "Needs from the
-- core"), paragraphs 7.1(6)(a) and (b) (piece ei1), 152.07(3)(a) and (b) (piece ei5) and the maximum numbers of weeks in
-- s 12(2) and s 152.14(1) are outside this piece: each is a plain input named for the fact.

§ `Regulation 11.1 - adjustment of the amount of self-employed earnings`

-- The adjustment is repeated every year from 2012 and compounds. One year's step is `reg 11.1(1) the adjusted amount for the
-- year`; the years chain through `reg 11.1(1) the adjusted amount after the years`. Whether the rounding of (3) is applied to
-- each year's amount before the next year's ratio is applied, or only to the last, is not said (fork F11 in NOTES.md): each
-- year's amount is rounded down and the next year's adjustment starts from the rounded amount.
-- src:201 | (2) If the ratio referred to in subsection (1) is less than 1.0 and would result in an adjusted amount of less than $6,000, the ratio is deemed to be 1.0.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 11.1(2) (src:201)
-- Plain gloss: a ratio below 1.0 does not cut the amount below $6,000; if it would, it counts as 1.0 and the amount stays as it was.
-- The ratio is the Act's paragraph 4(2)(b) ratio for the year (published figures; an input).
GIVEN `the amount before the year's adjustment` IS A NUMBER
      `the ratio referred to in paragraph 4(2)(b) of the Act` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `reg 11.1(2) the ratio after the floor of 1.0` IS
    IF     `the ratio referred to in paragraph 4(2)(b) of the Act` LESS THAN 1
       AND `the amount before the year's adjustment` TIMES `the ratio referred to in paragraph 4(2)(b) of the Act` LESS THAN 6000
    THEN 1
    ELSE `the ratio referred to in paragraph 4(2)(b) of the Act`

-- src:202 | (3) If the adjusted amount calculated in accordance with subsections (1) and (2) is not a multiple of one dollar, that amount shall be rounded down to the nearest dollar.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 11.1(3) (src:202)
-- Plain gloss: drop any part of a dollar.
GIVEN `the adjusted amount calculated in accordance with subsections (1) and (2)` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `reg 11.1(3) the adjusted amount rounded down to the nearest dollar` IS
    FLOOR `the adjusted amount calculated in accordance with subsections (1) and (2)`

-- src:200 | 11.1 (1) The amount of self-employed earnings referred to in subparagraph 152.07(1)(d)(i) of the Act shall be adjusted annually on a compound basis, beginning on January 1, 2012, by the ratio referred to in paragraph 4(2)(b) of the Act.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 11.1(1) (src:200)
-- Plain gloss: each year from January 1, 2012 the amount is multiplied by that year's ratio (with the floor of reg 11.1(2)) and
-- rounded down to the dollar (reg 11.1(3)). The amount before the first adjustment is the Act's figure in subparagraph
-- 152.07(1)(d)(i) (out of range: section 152.07 (piece ei5)); a caller passes the previous year's adjusted amount.
GIVEN `the amount before the year's adjustment` IS A NUMBER
      `the ratio referred to in paragraph 4(2)(b) of the Act` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `reg 11.1(1) the adjusted amount for the year` IS
    `reg 11.1(3) the adjusted amount rounded down to the nearest dollar`
        (`the amount before the year's adjustment`
         TIMES `reg 11.1(2) the ratio after the floor of 1.0` `the amount before the year's adjustment` `the ratio referred to in paragraph 4(2)(b) of the Act`)

-- src:200 | 11.1 (1) The amount of self-employed earnings referred to in subparagraph 152.07(1)(d)(i) of the Act shall be adjusted annually on a compound basis, beginning on January 1, 2012, by the ratio referred to in paragraph 4(2)(b) of the Act.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 11.1(1) (src:200)
-- Plain gloss: the adjusted amount after a run of years, the first ratio being the one for January 1, 2012 and the list running
-- year by year. An empty list means no adjustment has yet been made.
GIVEN `the amount of self-employed earnings referred to in subparagraph 152.07(1)(d)(i) of the Act` IS A NUMBER
      `the ratios referred to in paragraph 4(2)(b) of the Act, one for each year beginning with 2012, in order` IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `reg 11.1(1) the adjusted amount after the years` IS
    CONSIDER `the ratios referred to in paragraph 4(2)(b) of the Act, one for each year beginning with 2012, in order`
    WHEN EMPTY THEN `the amount of self-employed earnings referred to in subparagraph 152.07(1)(d)(i) of the Act`
    WHEN ratio FOLLOWED BY later THEN
        `reg 11.1(1) the adjusted amount after the years`
            (`reg 11.1(1) the adjusted amount for the year` `the amount of self-employed earnings referred to in subparagraph 152.07(1)(d)(i) of the Act` ratio)
            later

-- src:203 | 12 [Repealed, SOR/2016-206, s. 1]
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 12 (src:203)
`reg 12 repealed in this text` MEANS "reg 12: [Repealed, SOR/2016-206, s. 1]"

§ `Regulation 13 - the average number of weeks`

-- src:206 | 13 (1) For the purpose of paragraph 7.1(6)(b) of the Act, the average number of weeks of regular benefits of a claimant is the maximum number of weeks for which benefits may be paid to the claimant under section 8 of the Employment Insurance (Fishing) Regulations or subsection 12(2) of the Act, less the number of weeks of benefits paid to the claimant, including the weeks of benefits used to establish the overpayment under paragraph 7.1(6)(a) of the Act, with the result divided by two.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 13(1) (src:206)
-- Plain gloss: take the most weeks the claimant could have been paid (under section 8 of the Employment Insurance (Fishing)
-- Regulations or subsection 12(2) of the Act: out of range, piece ei1; the Fishing Regulations are not among this run's
-- inputs), subtract the weeks of benefits already paid (counting the weeks used to establish the overpayment under paragraph
-- 7.1(6)(a)), and halve the result. If more weeks were paid than the maximum the text does not say what the result is: NOTHING.
GIVEN `the maximum number of weeks for which benefits may be paid to the claimant under section 8 of the Employment Insurance (Fishing) Regulations or subsection 12(2) of the Act` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of weeks of benefits paid to the claimant, including the weeks of benefits used to establish the overpayment under paragraph 7.1(6)(a) of the Act` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `reg 13(1) the average number of weeks of regular benefits of a claimant` IS
    IF `the number of weeks of benefits paid to the claimant, including the weeks of benefits used to establish the overpayment under paragraph 7.1(6)(a) of the Act` AT MOST `the maximum number of weeks for which benefits may be paid to the claimant under section 8 of the Employment Insurance (Fishing) Regulations or subsection 12(2) of the Act`
    THEN JUST ((`the maximum number of weeks for which benefits may be paid to the claimant under section 8 of the Employment Insurance (Fishing) Regulations or subsection 12(2) of the Act` MINUS `the number of weeks of benefits paid to the claimant, including the weeks of benefits used to establish the overpayment under paragraph 7.1(6)(a) of the Act`) DIVIDED BY 2)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:207 | (2) For the purposes of paragraph 152.07(3)(b) of the Act, a self-employed person’s average number of weeks of benefits is the maximum number of weeks for which benefits may be paid to them under subsection 152.14(1) of the Act, less the number of weeks of benefits paid to them, including the weeks of benefits used to establish the overpayment referred to in paragraph 152.07(3)(a) of the Act, with the result divided by two.
@ref Employment Insurance Regulations reg 13(2) (src:207)
-- Plain gloss: the same for a self-employed person, using the maximum number of weeks in subsection 152.14(1) of the Act
-- (out of range: piece ei5) and the weeks used to establish the overpayment under paragraph 152.07(3)(a).
GIVEN `the maximum number of weeks for which benefits may be paid to the self-employed person under subsection 152.14(1) of the Act` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of weeks of benefits paid to the self-employed person, including the weeks of benefits used to establish the overpayment referred to in paragraph 152.07(3)(a) of the Act` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `reg 13(2) the average number of weeks of benefits of a self-employed person` IS
    IF `the number of weeks of benefits paid to the self-employed person, including the weeks of benefits used to establish the overpayment referred to in paragraph 152.07(3)(a) of the Act` AT MOST `the maximum number of weeks for which benefits may be paid to the self-employed person under subsection 152.14(1) of the Act`
    THEN JUST ((`the maximum number of weeks for which benefits may be paid to the self-employed person under subsection 152.14(1) of the Act` MINUS `the number of weeks of benefits paid to the self-employed person, including the weeks of benefits used to establish the overpayment referred to in paragraph 152.07(3)(a) of the Act`) DIVIDED BY 2)
    ELSE NOTHING
